## Logic Summary

1. Read the array size.
2. Create an array and read its elements.
3. Initialize `i` to the first index and `j` to the last index.
4. Swap `a[i]` and `a[j]` using a temporary variable.
5. Increment `i` and decrement `j`.
6. Repeat until the pointers cross.
7. Print the reversed array.

## Important Concepts

- **Array:** Stores multiple values of the same data type.
- **Index:** Position of an element in an array, starting from `0`.
- **Two-pointer technique:** Uses two indices to process elements from opposite ends.
- **Swapping:** Exchanges two values using a temporary variable.
- **Time complexity:** `O(n)` — each element is processed a constant number of times.
- **Auxiliary space complexity:** `O(1)` — reversal uses only a temporary variable and two pointers, excluding the input array.
